# Failed Logon Detection - SPL Queries

## 1. Find Failed Logons

```spl
index=main EventCode=4625
| table _time ComputerName Account_Name Account_Domain Logon_Type Failure_Reason IpAddress Status Sub_Status
| sort - _time
```

## 2. View Complete Failed-Logon Events

```spl
index=main EventCode=4625
| table _time ComputerName Message
| sort - _time
```

## 3. Count Failed Logons by Account

```spl
index=main EventCode=4625
| stats count by Account_Name ComputerName
| sort - count
```

## 4. Count Failed Logons by Source Address

```spl
index=main EventCode=4625
| stats count by IpAddress ComputerName
| sort - count
```

## 5. Investigate Failed Logon Types

```spl
index=main EventCode=4625
| stats count by Logon_Type
| sort - count
```

## 6. Investigate a Possible Brute-Force Pattern

```spl
index=main EventCode=4625
| bin _time span=5m
| stats count as failed_attempts values(Account_Name) as accounts by _time IpAddress ComputerName
| where failed_attempts >= 5
| sort - failed_attempts
```

This query identifies five or more failed-logon events grouped into a five-minute interval by source address and destination host.

**Note:** This is a starting point, not a definitive brute-force detector. Validate the extracted source IP field, tune the threshold to the environment, and account for legitimate authentication failures before treating a result as an alert.

## Expected Evidence

The search should identify Windows Security Event ID 4625 records and expose relevant account, logon, failure, and source-address information.

The supplied screenshots show five events in the Splunk search results.
